"""
Functional Competencies Question Bank: exactly 34 Easy, 33 Medium, 33 Hard = 100 questions.
Focus: Technical & Operational Capabilities, Process Mapping, SOPs, 5S Methodology, Lean Principles, ISO Standards, Mission Karmayogi (FRAC), and Quality Management.
"""

func_easy = [
    ("What are 'Functional Competencies' in human resource and organizational management?", "The specific technical knowledge, practical skills, and operational abilities required to perform a particular role or job function effectively", ["Universal personal personality traits like honesty and charisma", "The physical fitness exercises required for military recruits", "The general academic degrees awarded by secondary high schools"], "Functional competencies are role-specific operational capabilities (e.g., financial auditing, supply chain forecasting, civil engineering)."),
    ("In Lean manufacturing and workplace organization, what do the '5S' pillars stand for?", "Sort (Seiri), Set in Order (Seiton), Shine (Seiso), Standardize (Seiketsu), and Sustain (Shitsuke)", ["Select, Schedule, Supervise, Sanction, and Settle", "Speed, Strength, Stamina, Strategy, and Success", "Save, Sell, Source, Store, and Ship"], "Originating in Japan, 5S establishes visual workplace organization, cleanliness, standardization, and discipline to eliminate waste."),
    ("In the 5S methodology, what is the primary objective of the first step: 'Sort' (Seiri)?", "Eliminating all unnecessary items, obsolete tools, and clutter from the workspace", ["Arranging tools in alphabetical order", "Painting the office walls bright white", "Writing detailed daily work schedules for employees"], "Sorting removes non-essential clutter, freeing physical space and ensuring only tools needed for immediate work remain present."),
    ("In the 5S methodology, what does 'Set in Order' (Seiton) mean?", "Arranging necessary items neatly in designated locations so they are easy to find, use, and return ('a place for everything')", ["Throwing away all broken machinery parts", "Conducting a formal deep chemical cleaning of floors", "Signing attendance registers at shift start"], "Setting in order organizes tools logically near the point of use with clear visual labeling, slashing wasted searching time."),
    ("In the 5S methodology, what does 'Shine' (Seiso) involve?", "Cleaning the workplace and equipment thoroughly on a daily basis and inspecting for leaks, wear, or defects", ["Polishing the shoes of visiting corporate executives", "Turning on bright overhead fluorescent lighting", "Waxing the exterior of company delivery vans"], "Shining combines daily cleaning with preventative inspection, catching emerging mechanical malfunctions before breakdowns occur."),
    ("In the 5S methodology, what is the goal of 'Standardize' (Seiketsu)?", "Establishing standard operating procedures, visual checklists, and schedules to maintain the first three 5S practices", ["Banning all employee personal clothing in the office", "Forcing all company departments to produce identical outputs", "Standardizing the retail price of all commercial goods"], "Standardization embeds 5S routines into standard daily workflows, preventing regression into chaos and clutter."),
    ("In the 5S methodology, what does 'Sustain' (Shitsuke) mean?", "Fostering personal self-discipline, training, and continuous habits to keep the 5S standards alive long-term", ["Operating a factory without any managerial supervision", "Sustaining corporate profitability during economic recessions", "Maintaining identical staff headcount over 20 years"], "Sustaining is the hardest step, requiring cultural commitment, regular audits, and management coaching to prevent slipping."),
    ("What is an 'SOP' in operational and functional management?", "Standard Operating Procedure (a documented, step-by-step set of instructions to perform routine tasks consistently)", ["Senior Operations Planner", "System Optimization Protocol", "Standard Ownership Policy"], "SOPs ensure operational consistency, product quality, regulatory compliance, and smooth onboarding of new personnel."),
    ("What does 'SIPOC' stand for in business process mapping and Six Sigma?", "Suppliers, Inputs, Process, Outputs, and Customers", ["Strategy, Investment, Profit, Overhead, and Capital", "Standards, Inspection, Production, Orders, and Compliance", "Sourcing, Inventory, Packaging, Operation, and Cost"], "SIPOC provides a high-level visual summary of a process before undertaking detailed process improvement projects."),
    ("What does 'KPI' stand for in organizational performance monitoring?", "Key Performance Indicator", ["Knowledge Processing Interface", "Key Production Index", "Kernel Programming Instruction"], "KPIs are quantifiable measures evaluating how effectively an organization, team, or operational unit achieves core objectives."),
    ("What is 'SLA' in functional contract and service management?", "Service Level Agreement (a formal commitment defining expected service standards, uptime, quality, and turnaround times)", ["Standard Legal Action", "System License Authorization", "Strategic Logistics Allocation"], "An SLA sets measurable performance benchmarks (e.g., 99.9% server uptime, IT ticket resolution within 4 hours) between service provider and user."),
    ("In quality management, what is 'ISO 9001' globally recognized as?", "The international standard for Quality Management Systems (QMS)", ["An environmental sustainability certification for solar plants", "An occupational cybersecurity standard for banking software", "A safety certification for commercial passenger aircraft"], "ISO 9001 demonstrates that an organization consistently provides products and services meeting customer and regulatory requirements."),
    ("What is 'ISO 27001' in functional IT and enterprise governance?", "The international standard for Information Security Management Systems (ISMS)", ["A quality standard for food and agricultural processing", "A standard for manufacturing automotive passenger tires", "A certification for civil structural bridge engineering"], "ISO 27001 provides a systematic framework for managing sensitive company information, data privacy, and cyber security risks."),
    ("What is 'ISO 14001' in enterprise environmental governance?", "The international standard for Environmental Management Systems (EMS)", ["A certification for financial accounting software systems", "A quality standard for medical diagnostic devices", "A safety protocol for operating high-speed passenger trains"], "ISO 14001 helps organizations minimize their environmental footprint, reduce industrial waste, and comply with environmental laws."),
    ("What is 'ISO 45001' in industrial operations and human resources?", "The international standard for Occupational Health and Safety Management Systems (OHSMS)", ["A standard for corporate financial balance sheet audits", "A guideline for international maritime cargo shipping", "A certification for operating civilian drone flights"], "ISO 45001 aims to prevent work-related injuries, fatalities, and illnesses, fostering safe and healthy working environments."),
    ("What is a 'Process Flowchart' (Workflow Diagram) used for in functional operations?", "A visual diagram mapping the sequential steps, decisions, inputs, and outputs of an operational process", ["A financial chart showing annual company stock dividends", "An organizational chart showing who reports to whom", "A map showing physical geographic branch office locations"], "Flowcharts standardize understanding, identify unnecessary bottlenecks, and reveal redundant loops in business workflows."),
    ("In Lean operations, what is 'Muda'?", "A Japanese term for 'Waste' (any activity that consumes resources without creating value for the customer)", ["A term meaning continuous breakthrough innovation", "The physical uniform worn by factory assembly workers", "A financial bonus paid to top-performing employees"], "Lean methodology focuses relentlessly on identifying and eliminating Muda to maximize customer value and operational velocity."),
    ("How many classic types of 'Muda' (wastes) were identified by Taiichi Ohno in the Toyota Production System?", "7 Types of Waste (Transport, Inventory, Motion, Waiting, Overproduction, Overprocessing, and Defects)", ["3 Types of Waste", "12 Types of Waste", "5 Types of Waste"], "Eliminating the 7 wastes streamlines workflows, lowers manufacturing costs, and accelerates delivery cycle times."),
    ("In Lean operations, which type of waste is considered the most critical and damaging because it hides all other wastes?", "'Overproduction' (producing more than needed or before it is needed)", ["'Motion' (excessive walking by workers)", "'Waiting' (idle machine time)", "'Transport' (moving boxes between rooms)"], "Overproduction creates excess inventory, ties up capital, requires extra warehousing, and masks quality and defect issues."),
    ("What is 'Root Cause Analysis' (RCA) in functional quality problem-solving?", "A structured methodology used to identify the fundamental underlying failure that caused an operational defect or incident", ["A financial audit calculating money lost to workplace fraud", "A disciplinary interview to identify which worker to fire", "A botanical examination of agricultural plant tree roots"], "RCA focuses on eradicating the core systemic defect (root cause) rather than merely treating superficial surface symptoms."),
    ("What is the '5 Whys' technique in root cause analysis?", "Iteratively asking 'Why?' five times to peel away symptom layers and drill down to the fundamental root cause of a defect", ["Asking five different employees to explain what happened", "Giving employees five minutes to fix a broken machine", "Conducting five separate quality audits in a single year"], "Developed by Sakichi Toyoda, the 5 Whys uncovers the deeper systemic breakdowns that allowed an operational error to occur."),
    ("In inventory management, what does 'ABC Analysis' categorize inventory items by?", "Annual consumption value (A = High value/Low quantity, B = Moderate value, C = Low value/High quantity)", ["Alphabetical order of raw material chemical names", "Age of the suppliers who manufacture the goods", "Autonomous, Bureaucratic, and Commercial goods"], "ABC analysis applies the Pareto principle: strict control is maintained over the vital few 'A' items representing ~70-80% of value."),
    ("In inventory management, what does 'VED Analysis' classify maintenance spare parts by?", "Vital, Essential, and Desirable (based on the criticality of the part to continuous operational production)", ["Volume, Efficiency, and Durability", "Verified, Estimated, and Discarded", "Valuable, Expensive, and Discounted"], "Vital spares must be stocked at all times because their absence halts production entirely; Desirable spares cause negligible delay."),
    ("In inventory management, what does 'FSN Analysis' classify inventory by?", "Fast-moving, Slow-moving, and Non-moving items (based on inventory turnover and consumption rates)", ["Factory, Store, and Network goods", "Fixed, Standard, and Nominal items", "Full, Semi, and Null inventory"], "FSN analysis helps warehouse managers optimize physical storage placement and identify dead or obsolete stock for liquidation."),
    ("What is 'Just-in-Time' (JIT) in manufacturing and supply chain management?", "An operational methodology where materials and parts are delivered and produced exactly when needed, minimizing inventory", ["A policy where workers must arrive at the factory exactly on time", "Delivering all annual raw materials in a single massive shipment", "Producing goods 24 hours a day regardless of customer demand"], "Pioneered by Toyota, JIT eliminates costly storage warehouses, cuts carrying costs, and exposes hidden production inefficiencies."),
    ("What is 'Kanban' in Lean functional operations?", "A visual signaling system (cards, bins, or electronic signals) that regulates production and inventory flow based on actual pull demand", ["A Japanese martial arts self-defense training program", "A financial bonus paid to assembly line supervisors", "A written legal contract signed with raw material suppliers"], "Kanban prevents overproduction by authorizing the production or movement of parts only when a downstream signal is received."),
    ("What does 'CAPA' stand for in quality and compliance management?", "Corrective and Preventive Action", ["Central Auditing and Procurement Authority", "Cost Allocation and Pricing Analysis", "Compliance Assessment and Performance Appraisal"], "CAPA investigates non-conformances, implements immediate corrective fixes, and institutes preventative barriers to ensure no recurrence."),
    ("What is a 'Non-Conformance Report' (NCR) in quality auditing?", "A formal document recording that a product, service, process, or deliverable failed to meet specified quality standards", ["A letter rejecting a job applicant after an interview", "A report stating that an employee refused to work overtime", "A bank statement showing insufficient funds for checks"], "An NCR triggers CAPA protocols, documenting the deviation, root cause investigation, and required corrective disposition."),
    ("What is 'Value Stream Mapping' (VSM) in Lean operations?", "A visual flowchart illustrating every step involved in delivering a product/service, distinguishing value-adding from non-value-adding steps", ["A financial spreadsheet calculating annual corporate profits", "A geographic map showing customer retail store locations", "A timeline of executive career promotions over 10 years"], "VSM visualizes cycle times, wait times, and information flows, guiding redesigns to eliminate non-value-adding operational waste."),
    ("What is 'Benchmarking' in operational process improvement?", "Systematically comparing an organization's business processes and performance metrics against industry best practices or leading peers", ["Measuring the physical dimensions of wooden office workbenches", "Setting identical retail prices for all competing products", "Banning employees from looking at competitor websites"], "Benchmarking identifies capability gaps, stimulates competitive innovation, and establishes realistic operational improvement targets."),
    ("What is 'Change Over Time' (Setup Time) in factory operations?", "The time elapsed from the production of the last good unit of product A to the first good unit of product B on a machine", ["The time taken by an employee to change into work uniforms", "The time required to negotiate a fresh annual labor contract", "The interval between morning and afternoon tea breaks"], "Short setup times (SMED) enable flexible small-batch production, eliminate large inventory buffers, and boost responsiveness."),
    ("What is 'SMED' in Lean production?", "Single-Minute Exchange of Die (reducing equipment changeover setup times to under 10 minutes, i.e., single-digit minutes)", ["Standard Manufacturing Efficiency Directory", "Systematic Maintenance and Engineering Division", "Supply Management and Electronic Distribution"], "Formulated by Shigeo Shingo, SMED converts internal setup tasks to external setups, drastically slashing machine downtime."),
    ("What is 'Preventive Maintenance' (PM) in operational plant management?", "Regularly scheduled inspection, servicing, lubrication, and part replacement to prevent unexpected machine breakdowns", ["Repairing a machine only after it catastrophically explodes", "Purchasing brand new machinery every six months", "Banning workers from operating heavy manufacturing equipment"], "Preventive maintenance reduces costly unplanned downtime, extends asset lifespan, and preserves factory workplace safety."),
    ("What does 'FRAC' stand for in Mission Karmayogi's competency framework for Indian civil servants?", "Framework of Roles, Activities and Competencies", ["Functional Recruitment and Assessment Council", "Federal Rules for Administrative Compliance", "Framework for Revenue and Allocation Control"], "FRAC maps every civil service position to specific organizational roles, daily activities, and required behavioural, functional, and domain competencies.")
]

func_med = [
    ("Under Mission Karmayogi, what are the three distinct competency pillars defined in the FRAC framework?", "Behavioural Competencies, Functional Competencies, and Domain Competencies", ["Primary, Secondary, and Tertiary Competencies", "Administrative, Political, and Judicial Competencies", "Individual, Team, and National Competencies"], "FRAC establishes that public officials need universal behavioural values, operational functional skills, and specialized domain knowledge."),
    ("In functional quality management, what is 'Overall Equipment Effectiveness' (OEE)?", "A composite metric multiplying Availability, Performance rate, and Quality rate to measure true manufacturing productivity", ["The total purchase price of factory machinery divided by its weight", "The percentage of factory floor space covered by machinery", "The number of hours a machine runs without any electric power"], "OEE = Availability * Performance * Quality. A world-class OEE score is typically recognized at 85% or higher."),
    ("If a machine has 90% Availability, 80% Performance rate, and 95% Quality rate, what is its OEE?", "68.4% (0.90 * 0.80 * 0.95 = 0.684)", ["88.3%", "75.0%", "95.0%"], "OEE combines losses from downtime (Availability), speed slowdowns (Performance), and scrap/rework defects (Quality)."),
    ("In operational process engineering, what is 'Takt Time'?", "The maximum allowable time per unit to produce a product in order to precisely match the rate of customer demand", ["The physical cycle time of the fastest machine on the floor", "The time taken by delivery trucks to reach retail stores", "The total working hours of factory employees in a month"], "Takt Time = Available Production Time / Customer Demand. It sets the rhythmic operational pace for balanced production lines."),
    ("In operational process engineering, what is 'Cycle Time'?", "The actual time taken by an operator or machine to complete one cycle of an operation or produce one unit", ["The total calendar years a factory remains operational", "The time taken to conduct an annual financial audit", "The delivery transit time across international oceans"], "If Cycle Time exceeds Takt Time, bottlenecks emerge and demand cannot be met; if it is less, overproduction waste looms."),
    ("What is 'Lead Time' in supply chain and operations?", "The total elapsed time from the initial placement of an order by a customer to the final receipt of the finished deliverable", ["The duration of the daily morning production meeting", "The time taken by the project manager to approve invoices", "The warranty validity period printed on product boxes"], "Lead time includes order processing, procurement wait times, manufacturing cycle time, inspection, and logistics shipping."),
    ("In business process management, what is a 'Swimlane Diagram'?", "A process flowchart that visually distinguishes job responsibilities and sub-processes across departments or functional roles in parallel lanes", ["A blueprint of a competition Olympic swimming pool", "A timeline chart showing employee vacation leave periods", "A scatter plot analyzing worker typing speeds"], "Swimlanes clarify handoffs between departments (e.g., Sales, Credit, Operations), highlighting delays across functional boundaries."),
    ("In operational quality control, what is a 'Poka-Yoke' mechanism?", "A 'Mistake-Proofing' device or mechanism that prevents human errors from occurring or makes defects immediately obvious", ["A disciplinary fine imposed on workers who commit errors", "A financial bonus paid for working overtime shifts", "An automated robotic arm used for heavy industrial welding"], "Shigeo Shingo's Poka-Yoke uses physical shape constraints, sensors, or checklists so an operation cannot be assembled incorrectly (e.g., SIM card shape)."),
    ("In Lean operations, what does 'Andon' refer to?", "A visual signaling system (lights, audio alarms) that alerts operators and supervisors to an operational defect or problem on the line", ["A formal written letter terminating an employee", "A computer spreadsheet calculating annual depreciation", "A safety helmet worn inside industrial chemical plants"], "Andon boards provide immediate visual management; operators are empowered to pull the Andon cord to halt the line and fix defects immediately."),
    ("In Lean manufacturing, what does 'Gemba' (or Genba) mean?", "'The Real Place' (the actual physical workplace where value is created, e.g., the factory floor, hospital ward, or service counter)", ["The corporate executive boardroom in a skyscraper", "An external hotel where annual strategy retreats are held", "A legal courtroom where commercial contract disputes are heard"], "Lean leaders practice 'Gemba Walks': going to the front lines to observe real workflows, engage workers, and uncover operational facts."),
    ("In functional process optimization, what is 'Kaizen'?", "A philosophy of continuous, incremental improvement involving all employees from executive leadership to frontline workers", ["A sudden, radical organizational reorganization", "A massive multi-million dollar capital equipment purchase", "A policy of laying off underperforming contract workers"], "Kaizen emphasizes small, daily, low-cost enhancements that collectively compound into massive quality and productivity gains."),
    ("In project and operational risk management, what does 'FMEA' stand for?", "Failure Mode and Effects Analysis", ["Financial Management and Expenditure Audit", "Functional Milestone and Execution Assessment", "Factory Maintenance and Engineering Architecture"], "FMEA is a prospective structured technique to identify potential process failure modes, evaluate risks, and prioritize preventive fixes."),
    ("In FMEA, how is the 'Risk Priority Number' (RPN) calculated?", "RPN = Severity * Occurrence * Detection", ["RPN = Severity + Occurrence + Detection", "RPN = (Severity * Occurrence) / Detection", "RPN = Total Cost * Probability of Failure"], "High RPN items (high severity, high likelihood of occurrence, difficult to detect) receive top engineering priority for risk mitigation."),
    ("What does 'SPC' stand for in industrial manufacturing quality assurance?", "Statistical Process Control", ["Standard Production Capacity", "Systematic Procurement Contract", "Strategic Personnel Coordination"], "SPC uses statistical tools (control charts) to monitor process variability, distinguishing common cause variation from special cause defects."),
    ("In Statistical Process Control, what is the 'Rule of Seven' on a control chart?", "If seven consecutive data points fall on one side of the mean line, a non-random special cause is present and requires investigation", ["Every machine must be inspected exactly every seven days", "A process can have a maximum of seven defects per week", "Production lines must operate with seven operators"], "Even within control limits, a run of seven points signals an unnatural shift or trend in the process that cannot be ignored."),
    ("In quality assurance, what is 'Process Capability Index' (Cpk)?", "A statistical measure of how well a process can produce output within specified engineering customer tolerance limits", ["The maximum electrical voltage an assembly machine can handle", "The total number of units a factory produces in a 24-hour day", "The percentage of factory workers who hold technical diplomas"], "A Cpk of 1.33 or higher indicates that the process is capable, with the spread and center comfortably inside tolerance limits."),
    ("In inventory management, what does 'Stockout' mean?", "An operational situation where inventory demand cannot be met due to complete exhaustion of available stock", ["The annual physical auditing of warehouse merchandise", "Selling surplus factory equipment at public auctions", "Moving inventory boxes from ground floor to upper shelves"], "Stockouts halt production lines, lead to emergency expensive freight shipping, and damage client goodwill."),
    ("What is 'Safety Stock' (Buffer Stock) in inventory planning?", "Surplus inventory held as a protective cushion against unpredictable demand spikes or vendor delivery delays", ["Damaged merchandise set aside for return to suppliers", "Stock stored exclusively for fire safety emergency drills", "Inventory held permanently without ever being sold"], "Safety stock mitigates stockout risks, calculated using historical lead time variance and desired customer service levels."),
    ("In procurement operations, what is a 'Reorder Point' (ROP)?", "The inventory level that automatically triggers the placement of a new purchase order for replenishment", ["The date when old inventory is thrown into waste bins", "The maximum capacity of the physical warehouse building", "The price discount offered by vendors for prompt payments"], "ROP = (Daily Demand Rate * Lead Time in days) + Safety Stock. Reaching ROP prompts procurement action before stockouts occur."),
    ("What is 'Asset Tracking' in operational facility management?", "Monitoring the physical location, operational status, maintenance history, and ownership of capital equipment using RFID or barcodes", ["Calculating the annual tax depreciation of company vehicles", "Writing down equipment serial numbers on loose paper pads", "Locking all expensive machinery in underground vaults"], "Asset tracking prevents equipment loss, schedules timely preventive maintenance, and optimizes resource utilization across sites."),
    ("What is 'Total Productive Maintenance' (TPM)?", "A holistic equipment maintenance system aimed at achieving zero breakdowns, zero accidents, and zero defects through operator ownership", ["Outsourcing all equipment repair work to foreign agencies", "Replacing all factory machinery every three calendar years", "Operating machines until they break down completely before repairs"], "TPM champions 'Autonomous Maintenance' (Jishu Hozen), empowering frontline operators to perform daily cleaning, lubrication, and basic inspections."),
    ("What are the 'Six Big Losses' in Total Productive Maintenance (TPM)?", "Breakdowns, Setup/Adjustments, Idling/Minor Stops, Reduced Speed, Quality Defects, and Startup/Yield Losses", ["Taxes, Wages, Insurance, Rent, Utilities, and Advertising", "Theft, Fire, Flood, Strike, Bankruptcy, and Lawsuits", "Transport, Packaging, Storage, Delivery, Billing, and Auditing"], "The Six Big Losses categorize equipment productivity drains, serving as the targets for continuous OEE improvement."),
    ("What is 'Autonomous Maintenance' (Jishu Hozen) in TPM?", "Training and empowering equipment operators to perform routine maintenance, cleaning, lubrication, and minor inspections themselves", ["Robots maintaining and repairing other robots automatically", "Machines operating without any human beings present in the building", "Hiring external contractor mechanics for daily oil changes"], "Operator ownership fosters intimate familiarity with equipment, enabling early detection of abnormal vibrations or strange noises."),
    ("In Lean thinking, what is 'Heijunka'?", "'Production Leveling' (smoothing the volume and product mix of production over time to avoid spikes and idle valleys)", ["A technique for rapid visual inspection of electronic parts", "An annual bonus paid to workers for zero workplace accidents", "A method for recycling chemical industrial waste water"], "Heijunka prevents upstream supply chain bullwhip effects by producing small, leveled batches rather than huge erratic batches."),
    ("In operations management, what is the 'Bullwhip Effect'?", "Small fluctuations in retail consumer demand amplify exponentially into massive, erratic order swings upstream along the supply chain", ["A physical safety hazard caused by moving factory drive belts", "A sudden unexpected drop in national currency exchange rates", "A labor strike called by transportation truck drivers"], "Distorted forecasts, panic ordering, and batching create severe inventory shortages alternating with massive overstocking."),
    ("What is 'Cross-Docking' in modern logistics operations?", "Unloading incoming materials from inbound trucks and directly loading them onto outbound trucks with little or no intermediate storage", ["Storing pallets of goods in high-density warehouse racks for months", "Shipping cargo containers across international ocean borders", "Conducting customs inspections at maritime shipping ports"], "Cross-docking slashes warehouse holding costs, minimizes handling damage, and accelerates inventory turnover."),
    ("In business process re-engineering, what is 'BPR' (Hammer and Champy)?", "The fundamental rethinking and radical redesign of core business processes to achieve dramatic improvements in performance metrics", ["Minor incremental adjustments made to standard daily spreadsheets", "Replacing older computer keyboards with ergonomic keyboards", "An annual staff performance appraisal review cycle"], "BPR rejects obsolete legacy steps, rebuilding end-to-end workflows around customer outcomes rather than fragmented departmental tasks."),
    ("What is 'Design of Experiments' (DOE) in functional engineering quality control?", "A structured statistical method to simultaneously vary multiple input factors to determine their complex effects on an output parameter", ["Conducting random unscientific trial-and-error tests in factories", "Designing decorative aesthetic packaging boxes for retail products", "Testing consumer food products on volunteer human panels"], "DOE identifies optimal parameter settings and interaction effects rapidly with the minimum required number of experimental runs."),
    ("What is 'Taguchi Method' in robust quality engineering?", "Engineering methods that design products and processes to be robust and insensitive to environmental variations and component noise", ["Testing products until they are destroyed by high heat", "Using only the most expensive raw materials in manufacturing", "Inspecting 100% of finished products at the end of the line"], "Genichi Taguchi shifted quality focus upstream to offline product design, ensuring reliable performance despite manufacturing noise."),
    ("What is 'Kanban Bin System' (Two-Bin / Three-Bin System) in workshop stock replenishment?", "When the first bin is emptied, it acts as a visual purchase trigger for replenishment while operations consume the second reserve bin", ["Throwing empty plastic waste bins into municipal recycling", "Storing flammable chemicals in metal sealed containers", "Locking spare keys inside duplicate office safes"], "Two-bin systems prevent inventory runouts of common consumables (e.g., bolts, fasteners) with zero digital computer overhead."),
    ("In operational compliance, what is an 'Internal Audit'?", "An independent, objective assurance and consulting activity designed to add value and improve an organization's operations and controls", ["A criminal investigation conducted by federal police detectives", "A public inspection conducted by external tax revenue inspectors", "An informal conversation between colleagues during lunch"], "Internal audits evaluate internal control systems, risk management frameworks, and governance compliance systematically."),
    ("What is 'Corrective Action' versus 'Preventive Action' in quality systems?", "Corrective action eliminates the cause of an existing detected non-conformity; Preventive action eliminates causes of potential non-conformities", ["Corrective action is free; Preventive action requires cash fines", "Corrective action is done by workers; Preventive action by directors", "Corrective action applies to machines; Preventive action to people"], "Correction fixes the problem that occurred; prevention anticipates failure modes to ensure defects never emerge in the first place."),
    ("What is 'Visual Management' (Mieruka) in lean functional workplaces?", "Using visual cues (signs, floor lines, color codes, kanban boards) so anyone can instantly recognize normal versus abnormal conditions", ["Installing decorative art paintings on corporate office walls", "Mandating that all factory staff wear bright eyeglasses", "Using high-definition video projectors during team meetings"], "Visual management makes deviations and bottlenecks immediately obvious at a glance without having to consult complex databases.")
]

func_hard = [
    ("In statistical process control, what is the mathematical difference between 'Cp' and 'Cpk'?", "Cp evaluates process spread relative to specification limits; Cpk accounts for both process spread AND process centering relative to specs", ["Cp is for manufacturing; Cpk is for service industries", "Cp measures financial cost; Cpk measures physical weight", "Cp uses standard deviation; Cpk uses variance only"], "Cp = (USL - LSL) / 6*sigma. Cpk = min[(USL - Mean)/3*sigma, (Mean - LSL)/3*sigma]. If a process is off-center, Cpk is lower than Cp."),
    ("If USL = 110, LSL = 90, Process Mean = 103, and Standard Deviation (sigma) = 2, what is Cpk?", "1.17 (Upper limit Cpk = (110 - 103) / 6 = 1.167; Lower limit = (103 - 90) / 6 = 2.167; Cpk is the minimum)", ["1.67", "2.17", "0.85"], "Cpk reflects the closest distance to a specification limit: min[(110 - 103)/(3*2), (103 - 90)/(3*2)] = min[7/6, 13/6] = 1.167."),
    ("In Six Sigma methodology, what are the five sequential phases of the 'DMAIC' process improvement framework?", "Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, and Control", ["Design, Manage, Assess, Integrate, and Certify", "Discover, Model, Automate, Inspect, and Close", "Draft, Map, Audit, Implement, and Check"], "DMAIC provides a rigorous data-driven problem-solving roadmap to systematically identify root causes and sustain process improvements."),
    ("In the Six Sigma DMAIC framework, what is the primary objective of the 'Control' phase?", "Institutionalizing improvements through standardized procedures, training, monitoring plans, and statistical process control charts", ["Designing the initial project charter and business case", "Gathering baseline defect data from legacy databases", "Brainstorming potential root causes using fishbone diagrams"], "The Control phase anchors gains into daily standard operations, preventing processes from slipping back into old defective habits."),
    ("In Design for Six Sigma (DFSS), what is the 'DMADV' methodology applied to?", "Designing brand-new products or processes from scratch when no existing process meets customer requirements (Define, Measure, Analyze, Design, Verify)", ["Repairing broken machinery on existing factory assembly lines", "Auditing past corporate income tax returns for financial fraud", "Conducting annual employee performance salary reviews"], "DMADV (or IDOV) engineers new products with Six Sigma quality built in natively, preventing downstream manufacturing rework."),
    ("What does 'Total Quality Management' (TQM) define as its core operating philosophy (Deming, Juran, Crosby)?", "An organization-wide management approach centered on quality, continuous improvement, and customer satisfaction across all business functions", ["Inspecting finished products at the end of the line and rejecting defects", "Maximizing quarterly profits through aggressive marketing advertising", "Automating all manufacturing using high-speed industrial robotics"], "TQM involves every employee, shifts focus from post-production inspection to process prevention, and establishes a culture of quality."),
    ("In W. Edwards Deming's 14 Points for Management, what does 'Cease dependence on inspection to achieve quality' mean?", "Build quality into the product in the first place through robust design and process control, rather than screening out bad parts later", ["Abolish all quality departments and stop testing products completely", "Trust workers blindly without measuring any operational outcomes", "Rely entirely on foreign suppliers to guarantee part perfection"], "Deming proved that mass inspection is too late, ineffective, and costly; quality is created during production, not inspected in."),
    ("What does the 'Juran Trilogy' (Joseph M. Juran) define as the three core management processes for quality?", "Quality Planning, Quality Control, and Quality Improvement", ["Quality Design, Quality Manufacturing, and Quality Marketing", "Quality Auditing, Quality Sanctioning, and Quality Certification", "Quality Inception, Quality Execution, and Quality Decommissioning"], "The Juran Trilogy maps quality from initial design planning to operational control against standards, driving breakthrough improvement."),
    ("What was Philip Crosby's central thesis in 'Quality is Free'?", "Preventing defects is vastly cheaper than the costs of rework, scrap, warranties, inspections, and customer dissatisfaction", ["Quality products can be manufactured with zero financial expenditure", "Customers should receive all commercial products free of cost", "Advertising is the only investment required for corporate success"], "Crosby asserted that 'Zero Defects' is an achievable performance standard and that the 'Cost of Quality' is really the cost of non-conformance."),
    ("In the Cost of Quality (COQ) framework, what are the four distinct categories of quality costs?", "Prevention Costs, Appraisal Costs, Internal Failure Costs, and External Failure Costs", ["Direct Costs, Indirect Costs, Fixed Costs, and Variable Costs", "Material Costs, Labor Costs, Overhead Costs, and Tax Costs", "Capital Costs, Operational Costs, Administrative Costs, and Sales Costs"], "COQ demonstrates that investing in upstream Prevention (training, design) drastically shrinks catastrophic downstream Failure costs."),
    ("In the Cost of Quality framework, what is an 'External Failure Cost'?", "Costs incurred when a defective product reaches the customer (e.g., warranty claims, recalls, customer support, legal liability, lost goodwill)", ["Scrap and rework costs incurred inside the factory before shipping", "The cost of testing raw materials received from external suppliers", "The cost of training factory employees on machine safety rules"], "External failure costs are by far the most financially catastrophic, inflicting brand damage, litigation liabilities, and market share loss."),
    ("In queuing theory (operations research), what does 'Little's Law' state for a stable queuing system?", "L = Lambda * W (Average number of items in system = Average arrival rate * Average time spent in system)", ["L = Lambda / W", "L = Lambda + W", "L = (Lambda * W) / 2"], "Little's Law connects Work-in-Progress (WIP), Throughput, and Lead Time; reducing WIP directly shortens cycle lead time."),
    ("In operations research, what does the 'Theory of Constraints' (TOC - Eliyahu Goldratt) assert?", "Every operational system has at least one constraint (bottleneck) that limits its total throughput; managing the bottleneck maximizes output", ["Every machine in a factory must operate at 100% capacity at all times", "Costs must be reduced equally across all operational departments", "Hiring more workers always increases factory total revenue"], "TOC's 5 Focusing Steps (Identify, Exploit, Subordinate, Elevate, Repeat) concentrate resources on the single bottleneck that dictates total output."),
    ("In the Theory of Constraints (TOC), what is the 'Drum-Buffer-Rope' (DBR) scheduling methodology?", "The Drum is the bottleneck setting the pace; the Buffer protects it from starvation; the Rope synchronizes release of materials to the bottleneck", ["A musical band playing rhythmic music inside factory buildings", "A heavy mechanical rope used to pull damaged factory machinery", "A physical safety barrier preventing workers from touching conveyor belts"], "DBR coordinates plant operations so non-bottleneck processes never overwhelm or starve the critical capacity-constrained resource."),
    ("In operations management, what is 'OEE World-Class Benchmark' commonly defined as across global discrete manufacturing?", "OEE >= 85% (composed typically of 90% Availability, 95% Performance, and 99.9% Quality)", ["OEE = 100% (absolute mechanical perfection)", "OEE = 50%", "OEE = 70%"], "An 85% OEE reflects world-class discrete manufacturing efficiency, with minimal downtime, near-nameplate speed, and six-sigma quality."),
    ("In Lean process engineering, what is 'Standard Work' (Standardized Work)?", "The documented, agreed-upon current best practice sequence of operations that balances Takt Time, Work Sequence, and Standard In-Process Stock", ["A legal minimum wage contract signed with industrial labor unions", "A mandatory 40-hour work week enforced across all corporate branches", "A list of employee names arranged alphabetically in the human resources office"], "Standard Work provides a baseline for continuous improvement: without a recognized standard, genuine improvement is impossible."),
    ("In business process management, what is 'BPMN 2.0'?", "Business Process Model and Notation (a standardized graphical notation for modeling business processes and workflows)", ["A computer programming language used to code web browser engines", "A database management system for storing large multimedia video files", "A financial accounting standard for reporting international leases"], "BPMN 2.0 provides an unambiguous visual language bridging business process design with executable enterprise workflow engines."),
    ("What does 'Gemba Kaizen' contrast with regarding management location?", "It mandates that managers leave air-conditioned executive suites and go directly to the Gemba to observe reality with their own eyes", ["It advises managers to manage companies entirely through email reports", "It suggests hiring external consultants to make all operational decisions", "It recommends closing physical factories and outsourcing all manufacturing"], "Masaaki Imai showed that operational problems cannot be understood from spreadsheets; truth resides where value is physically created."),
    ("What is 'Hoshin Kanri' (Policy Deployment) in Japanese strategic operations management?", "A strategic planning methodology that cascades an organization's core long-term breakthrough objectives down into aligned tactical operational metrics", ["An annual martial arts competition conducted for corporate executives", "A method for calculating corporate income tax deductions on machinery", "A software program tracking employee daily computer keystrokes"], "Hoshin Kanri uses 'Catchball' (bidirectional dialogue) to align corporate vision with frontline operational projects and KPIs."),
    ("In Hoshin Kanri, what does the 'Catchball' process involve?", "A bidirectional dialogue between management and operational teams to negotiate targets, resource allocation, and realistic deployment plans", ["Playing athletic sports games during company outdoor corporate retreats", "Passing unfinished paperwork files between adjacent office cubicles", "Escalating all difficult customer complaints to the legal department"], "Catchball ensures strategy is not autocratically imposed from above, fostering operational alignment, feasibility, and shared ownership."),
    ("In high-volume manufacturing, what is 'Statistical Tolerance Stack-Up' (Root Sum of Squares - RSS)?", "A statistical method calculating the total variation of assembled components using square root of the sum of squared component tolerances", ["Adding all component maximum tolerance dimensions linearly (worst-case)", "Multiplying all component tolerance dimensions together", "Dividing the largest component tolerance by the smallest tolerance"], "RSS tolerance analysis avoids overly tight, expensive manufacturing tolerances by recognizing that random part variations rarely align in the worst-case extreme."),
    ("In supply chain network design, what is 'Facility Location Optimization' (Center of Gravity Method)?", "A mathematical technique locating a centralized distribution hub to minimize total transport costs based on customer volumes and geographic coordinates", ["Selecting a factory location based on where the company founder was born", "Building warehouses exclusively near coastal international ocean ports", "Locating offices in the geographic center of the national land mass"], "The Center of Gravity algorithm weights coordinates by freight volume to identify cost-optimal logistics warehouse nodes."),
    ("In inventory modeling, what is the 'Newsboy (Newsvendor) Model' used for?", "Determining optimal inventory order quantities for perishable, seasonal, or short-lifecycle goods with uncertain demand and salvage loss", ["Calculating the subscription pricing of daily printed newspaper deliveries", "Managing the delivery routes of local paper delivery vans in cities", "Auditing the annual advertising revenues of news media television channels"], "The Newsvendor model balances the cost of underage (stockout margin loss) against overage (spoilage/discount salvage loss)."),
    ("In maintenance engineering, what is the 'Bathtub Curve'?", "A visual failure rate curve showing high infant mortality, followed by low constant random failure rate, and rising wear-out failures", ["A plumbing diagram of a hotel bathroom drainage pipeline system", "A financial graph tracking company cash reserves over 20 years", "A scatter plot showing worker productivity versus room temperature"], "The bathtub curve guides reliability engineering: burn-in testing weeds out infant mortality, while preventive replacement forestalls wear-out."),
    ("In reliability engineering, what does 'MTBF' stand for?", "Mean Time Between Failures (the average elapsed operational time between inherent system failures of a repairable asset)", ["Maximum Temperature Before Fire", "Minimum Tolerable Budget Fraction", "Mandatory Testing Benchmark Frequency"], "MTBF = Total Operational Time / Number of Failures. High MTBF indicates high equipment engineering reliability."),
    ("In reliability engineering, what does 'MTTR' stand for?", "Mean Time to Repair (the average time required to troubleshoot, repair, and restore a failed asset to full operational status)", ["Maximum Time to Release", "Minimum Total Testing Rate", "Mandatory Transport Transit Route"], "MTTR evaluates operational maintainability and technician efficiency; shortening MTTR directly boosts overall system Availability."),
    ("What is 'Single Point of Failure' (SPOF) in operational system architecture?", "A critical component, system, or role whose failure will cause the entire operational system to collapse, lacking redundancy", ["A minor manufacturing defect in a non-essential plastic packaging cap", "A single employee who arrives five minutes late to work", "A software glitch that displays a typo on an internal memo"], "Resilient engineering eliminates SPOFs by incorporating parallel redundancy, failover mechanisms, and cross-trained backup capacity."),
    ("In enterprise operational safety, what is a 'Hazmat' (Hazardous Materials) protocol?", "Standardized procedures governing the safe storage, handling, transportation, labeling, and emergency response for hazardous chemicals", ["A software program that protects databases from malicious computer viruses", "An insurance policy that covers corporate executives from bankruptcy lawsuits", "A medical fitness examination required for office desk workers"], "Hazmat protocols (aligned with UN classifications and GHS) mitigate toxic spill, explosion, and chemical contamination risks."),
    ("What does 'GHS' stand for in chemical safety classification and labeling?", "Globally Harmonized System of Classification and Labelling of Chemicals", ["General Hazardous Safety Guideline", "Global Healthcare Standard", "Government Hazard Screening"], "GHS standardizes chemical hazard symbols, Safety Data Sheets (SDS), and warning labels internationally to protect workers."),
    ("In operational facility management, what is 'Ergonomics' (Human Factors Engineering)?", "The scientific design of workplaces, tools, and tasks to fit the physiological and cognitive capabilities of the human worker, preventing fatigue and injury", ["The study of corporate financial tax reduction optimization", "The aesthetic architectural decoration of luxury executive lobbies", "The mathematical calculation of computer network bandwidth speeds"], "Ergonomic design eliminates musculoskeletal strain, repetitive strain injuries (RSI), and cognitive fatigue, boosting sustained productivity."),
    ("What is 'Gemba Waste: Muri, Mura, and Muda' (The Three Ms of Lean)?", "Muda (Wasteful non-value activity), Muri (Overburdening machines or workers), and Mura (Unevenness or irregularity in production flow)", ["Money, Machinery, and Materials", "Modernization, Motivation, and Management", "Manufacturing, Marketing, and Maintenance"], "Lean targets all three Ms: Muri (overburden) causes breakdowns and accidents; Mura (unevenness) creates bottlenecks; Muda wastes resources."),
    ("What is 'Root Cause Corrective Action' (RCCA) institutionalization benchmark?", "A systemic engineering or process design change that makes recurrence of the root cause physically or procedural impossible", ["Posting a warning sign telling workers to 'Be more careful next time'", "Issuing a verbal reprimand to the machine operator who made the mistake", "Filing the incident report in a drawer and waiting to see if it happens again"], "True institutionalization relies on hard architectural barriers (Poka-Yoke, system validations) rather than unreliable human memory."),
    ("In public administration competencies under Mission Karmayogi, what is a 'Competency Dictionary'?", "A centralized repository detailing competency titles, descriptions, proficiency levels (1 to 5), and observable behavioral indicators", ["A standard printed English language dictionary stored in public libraries", "A list of government acronyms used across central ministries", "A database of all civil servants' home addresses and phone numbers"], "The Competency Dictionary standardizes public service role profiles, training needs assessments, and career progression pathways.")
]
